          flip, rotation >>29&7, sil/snapshot gates, maskpost frac,
          drawfuzz, R_GetColumn wrap, pool overflow, calcheight).
          USER SNAPSHOT (F1): at the user's request, a gameplay
          snapshot for offline replay of glitch frames.  F1 during
          play (also demo) writes doomsnapN.log in the emu cwd after
          present: SNAP header (tic, w/h/proj/centery), CAM (view
          xyz/angle/cos/sin), MOBS (all thinkers: typ/xyz/angle/
          sprite/frame/flags/health/state/radius/floorz/subsector
          heights+light), VIS (vissprite pool), DS (drawseg pool:
          seg verts/linedef/side/x-range/scale basis/sil heights/
          light/mid+tmidbase), DST (per-seg clip snapshot over its
          range), DSM (masked col/flag), SPTOP/SPBOT/SOLID (full
          width clip arrays), FB8 (framebuffer as hex rows for
          pixel-level sprite-vs-wall analysis).  view->snapshot()
          (view.b/view.m) + F1 binding (doom.b keysym/handlekey,
          edge-triggered, game continues).  Tested headless via
          XTEST F1 injection: 3 taps -> 3 logs with tic/pos/angle
          confirmations; content parsed and validated (92 mobjs,
          15 vis, 43 segs, 200 hex rows at spawn).
          FRAME-HISTORY REVIEW (F2): for 1-frame glitches too fast to
          catch with F1.  Every render captures full state into a
          preallocated 35-slot ring (zero per-frame allocation):
          camera, fb8, vissprites, drawseg scalars + packed clip
          slices, compact mobjs (sector as index+heights+light),
          final solid clips.  F2 freezes the sim and reviews the
          ring: Left/Right step (tap) or scroll (hold), F1 snapshots
          the shown frame to doomsnapN.log, F2/Esc resumes live.
          view->snapshot() refactored to capture()+snapshotfrom()
          shared by live F1 and ring; SPTOP/SPBOT omitted from ring
          files (per-sprite scratch, re-derived offline).  Validated
          headless (auto snapshot+ringsnap+ringrestore at tic 100,
          then removed): ring retains 35 frames, files parse with
          existing tools, basis oracle clean.  Mid stored as texture
          index (name resolved at emit); sector as index via
          height/light/flat tuple match.
          BUG FIX (ROOT CAUSE of the sprite glitches - user snapshots
          doomsnap0-2): addline never did vanilla R_AddLine's
          backface cull (r_bsp.c:265-276, span >= ANG180).  Backfacing
          twins of visible walls entered the drawseg pool (41 of ~110
          slots in every snapshot) and their clip snapshots hid
          sprites standing geometrically in front (gibbed-marine
          corpse fully/partially invisible in snaps 0/1 via backfacing
          seg 293 while twin seg 320 was correctly stored+skipped),
          or opened rows showing sprites through walls (snap2 pillar
          base cols 189-197).  Offline drawspscan replay from the
          snapshot data matches the engine bit-for-bit and predicts
          the fix (corpse columns all open post-cull).  Fix: span =
          angle1-angle2 via gmod->pointtoangle2 (bit-exact vanilla
          tantoangle table, verified zero table-vs-atan disagreements
          on snap0/2 and 4 boundary keeps on snap1); return when
          signed span < 0 (== u32 span >= ANG180).  Also verified:
          map.b preserves SEGS lump vertex order (side test inputs
          genuine); vanilla R_ScaleFromGlobalAngle degenerates to
          64*FRACUNIT for such oblique segs so vanilla culls rather
          than clips - matching our cull.  A/B on deterministic
          DEMO1: sim identical, PROJ identical, clip checker clean
          (80396 lines, 0 violations), pool 256->~20.  USER-CONFIRMED
          FIXED.
          BUG FIX (1-frame left-edge wall glitch at grazing corners -
          user report: texture intact, wrong offset/depth for 1 frame
          at stairs, snaps back): the dd depth clamp
          (dd = max(d, FRACUNIT/4)) tilted the scale basis when a wall
          endpoint sat microscopically in front (0 < d < 0.25): ox
          stayed at the true exploded projection while s maxed at the
          clamp, so on-screen columns near that end rendered at up to
          4x wrong depth.  Proven vs raycast ground truth on the
          user's glitch frame (E1M1 seg 303, endpoint depth +0.039u):
          old basis gives 23-50% of true scale across the span, fixed
          basis matches truth to 0.2% every column.  Fix: extend the
          near-plane clip from d <= 0 to d < FRACUNIT/4 (snap to the
          0.25 crossing, keeping (ox,s) on the true perspective line;
          dropped sliver is always off-screen); skip the seg when
          both ends are inside (degenerate, unreachable past 16u
          collision radius).  Handoff at d = 0.25 is seamless (snapped
          frac = 0).  Validated: seg-basis oracle updated to the new
          formulas; Python simulation of the new branch with
          engine-exact arithmetic reproduces raycast truth; normal
          frames unaffected (no endpoint in the slab -> identical
          code path); build clean, smoke clean.  USER VISUAL
          CONFIRMATION PENDING at stair/door corners.
          REMOVED (user request): demo playback (-demo1, desynced),
          gametest (-g), and the other dev-test modes (-d/-t/-r/-m/-p
          with videotest/rendertest/maptest/walltest).            Diagnostics
          stay: F1 snapshot + F2 ring review + -v (PDROP/PROJ/CMISS/
          POFF/TRIP).  usage is now: doom [-v] [doom.wad].
          RING BUFFER 35 -> 105 frames (~3s) at user request, for
          catching rare glitches; masked clip pool 2048->1024,
          CLIPPOOL 8192->4096 (still 3x observed max; overflow marks
          frame invalid, never corrupts); review hold-scroll sped up
          (every 3rd iter).
          SLIDE BUMP INVESTIGATION (user snapshots doomsnap0-4: 1-tic
          x-stick sliding past E1M1 line 148): VERDICT vanilla-correct
          Doom physics, no port bug.  Evidence: full pipeline diffed
          clean vs vanilla source (xymovement half-step, trymove/
          checkposition/pitcheckline heights, boxonlineside/
          pointonlineside, lineopening, slopediv/pointtoangle2/tanto-
          angle/finesine tables, approxdistance, pointondvlineside,
          fixeddivsat saturation, interceptvector incl. den==0 guard
          (vanilla has it too), slidetraverse side/block rules,
          hitslidelined deltaangle/angle tables, slidemove
          fudge/retry/stairstep control flow, ST_*/ML_*/FRICTION/
          STOPSPEED/GRAVITY/MAXMOVE consts, map.b bbox/slopetype/dxdy/
          front-back sectors); Limbo big-div proven truncating (C-like)
          via on-target probe program; exact offline replay reproduces
          the incident step-for-step (graze intercept frac 0.047 on
          a 0.00003u corner nick -> flush trymove fails -> stairstep-y
          with full momy) and agrees between vanilla min-frac and
          port index-order traversal (line 148 nearest on its ray in
          both).            Incidental notes, NOT changed: ticcmd tuning is
          always-run (fm 50, sm 25, at 1280, no 640 slow-turn ramp)
          vs vanilla walk/run/speed-key tables - a feel/design
          decision, not a physics bug; diagonal slide angles differ
          accordingly.
          WALK/RUN SEMANTICS (user request, revised twice): first
          implemented vanilla G_BuildTiccmd tables (forward {25,50},
          strafe {24,40}, turn {640,1280} with 320 slow-turn ramp,
          Shift as run key); user then asked to DIVERGE toward modern
          ports (confirmed: Chocolate/Crispy/PrBoom keep the ramp,
          GZDoom-family does not).  Final: always-run, no speed key,
          no ramp - forward 50, strafe 40, instant turn 1280, both
          axes clamped to +-MAXPLMOVE.  Direct strafe keys (A/D/Q/E)
          and arrows keep their roles.  No sim changes (game.b
          consumes raw cmd values).  Controls print updated.
          BOOM SLIDE-BOUNCE FIX (user request + Doom Wiki motto,
          approved divergence): vanilla P_HitSlideLine reverses
          momentum outright on exactly-anti-parallel slides
          (deltaangle == ANG180 hits the strict > comparison,
          cos = -1; routine on axis-aligned walls).  Ported Boom's
          fix verbatim (killough 3/2/98, kept by PrBoom+/DSDA):
          moveangle += 10 before differencing, no
          demo_compatibility switch (no demos kept).  Validated
          numerically with exact tables: anti-parallel slides go
          from full reversal to correct forward slide; broad sweep
          33/400000 outputs differ (boundary slivers only).
          REVERTED (user request): both the Boom moveangle += 10 and
          the stairstep subdivision are out again - slide physics is
          back to vanilla-exact.  The momentum bumps persist and the
          user declined further tracing; vanilla behavior stands.
          ANIMATED WALL/FLAT TEXTURES (user request): vanilla
          P_InitPicAnims + P_UpdateSpecials animate block (p_spec.c).
          animdefs table transcribed exactly (22 entries, verified
          against source); frames are contiguous index ranges in
          TEXTURE1 order / flat WAD order.  Rendering resolves names
          through texanim/flatanim identity-plus-animation maps
          (rtexture/rflat), so wall/plane code is untouched; per-tic
          advance pic = base + ((leveltime/speed + i) % numpics),
          formula pinned offline (8-tic phases).  Shareware WAD check:
          only NUKAGE1-3 present and adjacent (flat indices 50-52),
          so init yields exactly 1 anim; everything else skips
          cleanly (vanilla would I_Error on bad cycles - we skip).
          Init at end of Renderer.init, advance from game ticker
          (milestone-13 P_UpdateSpecials territory noted).  Build
          clean, smoke clean.  USER VISUAL CONFIRMATION PENDING
          (nukage flow).
          SCROLL-LEFT WALLS (user report: TEKWALL1 on the E1M1 armor
