          WALL-TOP CLIP BUG, E1M1 COURTYARD (user snapshot
          doomsnap0.log: top ~33 rows of the wooden building wall
          missing, sky in their place).  Reproduced pixel-exact
          with a temp warp to the snapshot camera in a fresh level
          (DS records identical, framebuffer boundary profile
          identical), temp code fully reverted after.  Root cause
          in drawtwosided's clip advance: the portal column clip
          (cclip) advanced to the FRONT ceiling row, but vanilla's
          yl comes from topfrac which uses the SKY-HACKED worldtop
          (= BACK ceiling when both ceilings are sky).  The
          sec20|sec22 gateway portal (front ceil 24, back ceil 64,
          both F_SKY1) closed rows down to the 24u line while
          vanilla leaves them open to the 64u line, beheading the
          wooden wall drawn behind the portal.  One-line-class fix:
          fctop now uses bceilz when both ceilings are sky (same
          condition the upper-texture skip already used).  Verified:
          wall-top boundary now matches the placement formula
          within +-1 row across the whole span (was 30-40 rows
          short), full-frame diff vs pre-fix bounded to x 107-229
          y 0-60 (the bug area only, 3986 px, no collateral),
          rebuild clean, no emu left running, user's snapshot file
          restored untouched.
          BROWN-BAND ABOVE WALL, E1M1 COURTYARD SECOND REPORT
          (user snapshot, camera 2376,-3536 angle 232): investigated
          as a possible second wall-top clip bug.  VERDICT: NO BUG,
          vanilla-exact rendering.  Evidence chain: (1) reproduced
          the frame pixel-identically with a temp camera warp in a
          fresh level (DS records + fb8 identical; temp code fully
          reverted, doom.b byte-clean).  (2) No-planes isolation
          run shows the wall loop draws the top exactly at the
          formula row (wood from row 51 = yt, black above) - wall
          code innocent.  (3) Painter bisection (disable sky only)
          + unconditional span instrumentation: no floor/mapplane
          span touches the band; the only painter is the sky-column
          path.  (4) In-texture proof: the renderer's SKY1 column
          for those screen columns holds palette indices
          106,109,78,109 at rows 47-50, and the WAD's SKY1 patch
          bytes at the same place hold the identical brown
          mountain pixels - the brown band IS SKY1's brown
          mountains abutting the wall top, with the sloping
          mountain silhouette reading as a cut wall line.  (5) The
          composition path (negative patch origin oy=-8, source not
          advanced on top clip) matches doomgeneric
          R_DrawColumnInCache line-for-line - including the quirk,
          so placement is vanilla-identical too.  Lesson recorded:
          E1 SKY1 has brown mountains that sit directly above wall
          tops in the courtyard; check WAD bytes before assuming a
          clip bug.  No code changed for this one (the earlier
          fctop sky-hack fix stands, separate issue).
          PHANTOM PLANE SPANS OVERPAINTING WALL TOPS (user snapshot:
          courtyard exit walls cut short, toggled by drawplanes -
          user was right, second real bug in the same area).  The
          two-sided markplane calls ran UNGATED, but vanilla gates
          them on markceiling/markfloor (r_segs.c:219+).  A shared-
          plane portal (both sky, same light) recorded a sky span
          down to its front-ceiling row; drawplanes paints spans
          after all walls, so the span smeared sky over the wooden
          wall drawn behind the portal (E1M1 gateway: span bottom
          row 66 over wall top row 51).  Fix: gate both markplane
          calls on markceiling/markfloor.  Verified by pixel oracle
          (new wall1s.py for 1-sided mids): 0 mismatches across the
          full wall at 3 columns (was 8-16 mismatched top rows);
          phantom sky spans gone from the snapshot (only the wall's
          own abutting sky span remains); first snapshot's wall
          still 0 mismatches (no regression).  Complements the
          earlier fctop sky-hack fix (right edge row when
          markceiling=1; this fix skips the mark entirely when 0).
          1-sided marks correctly stay ungated (vanilla marks
          terminal walls unconditionally).
