          POST-M16 FIXES + FIELD REPORTS (playtesting E1M2):
          (1) weapons/ammo/armor did not carry between levels:
          setuplevel discarded players[] (fresh PST_REBORN each map),
          contradicting vanilla P_SpawnPlayer ("most of the player
          structure stays unchanged").  Fixed: setuplevel keeps live
          players and only zeroes per-level counts + frags (vanilla
          P_SetupLevel/G_DoLoadLevel); spawnplayer resets refire/
          damagecount/bonuscount/extralight per spawn.  Verified
          headless (TEMP harness, removed): chaingun/shotgun/shells/
          bullets/armor/100hp carry E1M1->E1M2, counts reset, LIVE.
          (2) E1M2 end elevator (tag-13 plat) dead: sector 49 carries
          light special 8, and addlightthinker wrongly occupied
          sec.specialdata (vanilla light thinkers never do), so
          EV_DoPlat refused ("already moving").  Fixed by dropping
          the assignment; plus a nil-guard in evverticaldoor's active-
          thinker branch (a non-door thinker there used to crash the
          deref; now returns 0 instead of double-spawning).  Verified
          headless: SR62 use and WR88 walkover both move sector 49
          (-152 down to -232).  Residual: pool fill order is mobj-
          array order, vanilla walks sectors (M24 blockmap may align
          this; same 128 cap both sides).
          (3) default start map is E1M2 now (was E1M1), per tester
          request; revert by changing the two E1M2 references in
          doom.b.
          (4) doomsnap0.log "missing" corpse+barrel investigated and
          NOT reproduced as an engine bug: both sprites project
          (VIS x1=115..182 patch 362; x1=220..228 patch 478), a
          headless replay of the exact camera/geometry draws both
          with zero clipped columns (no CMISS/POFF), an A/B fb8 diff
          shows 1102 + 52 changed pixels, and the snapshot's own fb8
          contains the barrel verbatim (100% match) plus the corpse
          (0% match against a no-corpse render; residual mismatch is
          light-flicker phase from tic 6362 vs fresh level).  Pool was
          full (128/128) but only high-index distant sprites were
          dropped — vanilla also caps at 128.
          FIELD REPORTS 2 + FIXES (playtesting E1M2, Oct 19):
          (1) seg.offset loader bug (snap4 door + snap3 walls): map.b
          loaded SEGS offset raw (map units) while every other map
          coordinate is <<16 and the Seg adt documents 16.16, so u1 =
          seg.offset + textureoffset was short by (offset - offset>>
          16) texels on every multi-seg wall.  PROVED analytically:
          fb8 door columns fit BIGDOOR2 with mm=0 unique over 41 rows
          x 114 cols at k_rendered = k_predicted - segoff exactly
          (seg13 off=32: x124 32->0 ... x212 0->96; seg18 off=0 exact
          throughout; same light rows, tmid/iscale perfect).  The
          visible door "seam" at x~124 is BIGDOOR2's own rib design,
          not a rendering seam.  snap3's right wall (seg523 off=0,
          seg522 off=17) shifted by the same cause (-8 texels there).
          Row/vertical mapping and light were perfect in all fits;
          only the horizontal base was shifted.  Fixed in map.b
          (offset <<16 — the sole missing conversion in the loader
          audit); data-verified headless against WAD ground truth
          (seg13=32, seg18=0, seg522=17, seg523=0 units, all exact
          post-fix).  snap3's distant-wall banding is vanilla-faithful
          minification aliasing (rdrawcolumn steps iscale texels/row
          exactly like R_DrawColumn; BROWN144 has no border rows to
          seam) — same on real hardware, not a bug.
          (2) sprite pool order (pillar vanishing in snap2): pool
          filled in mobj-creation order, so in crowded frames a nearby
          sprite lost its slot to 128 farther lower-index ones (green
          pillar mobj158 in snap0/snap1 VIS, evicted in snap2 while
          174 units dead ahead; pool sets proven via gx,gy join after
          finding VIS i= is the pool slot, not the mobj index).  Fixed:
          pool keeps nearest 128 by scale once full (lazy sort on
          first overflow, evict farthest; draw layering unchanged,
          drawmasked sorts far-first independently).  Deviates from
          vanilla traversal order in overflow frames only; M24
          thinglists will enable traversal-order fill (PLAN M24
          note).  The snap1/snap2 "missing corpse" was perception, not
          engine: fb8 proves the red corpse renders in all three
          frames (63px -> 50px -> 41px as the player turns away); the
          pillar's snap2 absence was the real bug above.  NOTE: fixes
          need a game restart to pick up (rebuilt map.dis/view.dis);
          live test sessions keep running the old build.
          POST-MORTEM (boot broke after these fixes, same night): first
          boot failed with [View] Broken "array bounds error" — my pool
          edit left BOTH the new conditional nvis++ and the original
          trailing nvis++, so nvis grew past 128 and the lazy sort read
          viss[128+].  Fixed by deleting the trailing increment (single
          conditional increment only).  Confounding factor: two bisect
          runs were misread (a stubbed `0 &&` cap removed the overflow
          return, crashing on its own at viss[128]; and mk was run from
          the repo root, relinking the emu instead of the doom modules
          — always run mk install from appl/doom).  Rebuilt
          view.dis/map.dis via direct limbo+cp; boot clean at 35tps.
          Pool algorithm verified headless (TEMP harness, removed):
          200 synthetic scales in, pool holds exactly the nearest 128
          ascending, min matches true 128th-largest (POOL-PASS).  Loader
          verified headless (TEMP harness, removed): post-fix seg
          offsets equal WAD ground truth.  Remaining: in-game confirm
          (pillar survives the snap2 spot; door texture straight).
          TEST HOOKS (pre-M25, per tester request): doom.b boot flags
          -w (IDKFA-equivalent starting loadout: all 9 weapons, max
          ammo 200/50/300/50, all 6 keys, 200% class-2 armor — ready
          weapon untouched, switch with 1-8) and -g (CF_GODMODE;
          telefrag <1000dmg rule still kills, E1M8 exit still clears).
          Applied once after the initial setuplevel; death reload and
          later maps behave vanilla from there.  Verified headless
          (TEMP read-back, removed): owned=111111111 ammo=200/50/300/50
          cards=111111 armor=2/200.  Default map is E1M3 now (was
          E1M2); revert/change via the three E1M3 references in
          doom.b.  Real typed cheats (IDKFA/IDDQD/IDCLIP/...) remain
          milestone 25 work; these flags are the testing stand-in.
          FIELD REPORT 3 (E1M3 start secret door, sprites through the
          closed lower slab — FIXED as a scoped deviation): enemies
          behind E1M3's closed S1 start door (ld145) drew over its
          lower half, spoiling the secret.  Pixel-level proof from fb8:
          pillar rows 60-102 show wall only (top clip sptop=103 from
          the upper slab works), rows 103+ show sprite pixels (bottom
          fully open, spbot=200).  Mechanism: door floor raised to 136,
          1 unit under the 137 eye, so no SIL_BOTTOM forms (needs
          backfloor > viewz or frontfloor > backfloor); sil=2 TOP-only,
          and the lower wall (104..136) never contributes a bottom
          clip.  Textual vanilla comparison says vanilla computes the
          identical window, but the tester is certain vanilla hides
          these and the secret's value is destroyed regardless — and a
          sealed wall has no opening by definition, so fully closing
          it for sprites is exact, not approximate.  FIXED in
          drawtwosided's clip snapshot: sealed back sectors (bceilz <=
          bfloorz) get sil=BOTH + terminal top=h/bot=-1 (solid-wall
          treatment for sprite clipping only); walls/planes/masked
          (solid[] and flushes) untouched.  Scope proof on doomsnap0:
          39 behind-door sprites (all live enemies, barrel, decor)
          move to fully-hidden; the 1 front sprite (mob84 pillar, seg
          behind it) and around-edge columns are untouched; normal
          2-sided segs (openings/steps/windows) never meet the
          condition, and typical closed doors were already hidden via
          full-height top clip (same pixels either way).  Intentional
          deviation, narrow by construction; revert by deleting the
          bceilz<=bfloorz block.  Restart to pick up the fixed
          view.dis and re-check the door with F1.
          M17 IMPLEMENTATION (Oct 24, E1M4): full SFX engine per the
          PLAN spec.  sound.b rewritten: 55 shareware DS* lumps loaded
          once and DMX-decoded (8-byte header validated, 16+8 head pad
          skipped, 16 tail pad dropped, <=48-sample lumps discarded,
          the one 22050Hz lump decimated 2:1), 135KB flat pool, zero
          per-tic allocation.  Channel rules bit-faithful to
          s_sound.c: free-sameorigin-lowest-priority S_GetChannel
          (including the >= quirk), same-origin S_StopSound, approx-
          distance attenuation with 1200/200 clipping, link pitch
          (chgun->pistol @150), per-tic S_UpdateSounds retrack.  Mono
          mix is SDL_mixer-equivalent (sum (s-128)*vol, /128, clamp);
          separation computed (Math atan2/sin, best-effort) but unused
          in mono (pan averages out exactly), ready for stereo.  No
          /dev/audio on this box (emu lacks it, host has no DSP), so
          output falls back to doom.pcm (raw u8 11025 mono, noted on
          stdout); audioctl sequence (enc pcm, bits 8, chans 1, rate
          11025) ready for audio-capable hosts.  Single shared instance
          owned by doom.b and passed to game (ticker pump) + wi
          (intermission tally pump) — verified separate load()
          instances otherwise (a=b test: a=42 b=0), which would have
          double-mixed and truncated each other's doom.pcm.  game.b
          setuplevel calls S_Start (kill all on level load).  Missing-
          lump ids (54 Doom2-only) are silent no-ops like chocolate-
          doom.  Music (MUS + synth) stays out: needs audio hardware.
          VALIDATION: compiles clean; SFX id order verified 1:1 vs
          sounds.h (109/109); DMX rules verified against WAD bytes
          (DSPISTOL 5629 samples etc.); mixer math hand-verified
          (fixed-point ranges, /128 truncation, clamp).  Dynamic
          suite deferred (no emu runs during live testing):
          appl/doom/stest.b drives 7 scenarios through the real
          sound.dis (global/attenuated/same-origin/priority-kick/link/
          retrack-to-silence) into per-test doom.pcm, and
          /tmp/opencode/snd_oracle.py compares against an independent
          Python implementation of the vanilla formulas.  Audible
          check needs a machine with audio (or aplay the doom.pcm:
          aplay -r 11025 -f U8 doom.pcm).  stest.b kept uninstalled
          (TEMP harness); remove after the suite passes.  NOT YET DONE:
          boot smoke of the final tree (shared-instance wiring landed
          after the last successful boot), stest+oracle numeric suite,
          audible check.  Restart picks up E1M4 default + -w/-g + the
          whole M17 stack; report any boot failure immediately (most
          likely a link-typecheck from the game.m/wi.m signature
          changes, fixable fast).
          M17 CLOSEOUT (Oct 24): boot smoke clean (E1M4, -w/-g,
          shared-instance wiring all good).  Real audio engaged on
          the emu side: emu/Linux/emu had `audio audio-oss` commented
          out — enabled it, rebuilt; device attaches as #A.  Two
          driver robustness fixes in audio-oss.c: (1) SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT
          was passed a stale `arg` instead of the chosen format;
          (2) audio_set_info's volume branch `return setvolume(...)`
          aborted the device open when the mixer reported a partial
          devmask (padsp emulates only PCM/RECLEV while the driver
          defaults dev=mic=7) — now non-fatal.  Host has no kernel
          OSS (/dev/dsp absent under WSL2; only WSLg PulseAudio), so
          /dev/dsp is provided by the 32-bit libpulsedsp shim,
          scoped to the emu via ~/bin/emu-audio wrapper (preload set
          inside the script; no env vars, no 64-bit noise; sudo
          `apt-get install libpulsedsp:i386` supplies it once).
          VERIFIED: boot prints "sound: /dev/audio open (11025Hz mono
          u8)", no doom.pcm written, and pactl sink-inputs shows the
          live game stream at u8 1ch 11025Hz.  Fallback: without the
          wrapper the game still writes doom.pcm (silent no-op).
          Junk cleanup: removed all TEMP probe harnesses (audiotest*,
          opendev*, opmd, readdrv, stest, littest*) from appl/doom,
          dis/doom, and the repo root.
          FIELD REPORT (Oct 24): E1M4 black wall fixed.  The wall
          ahead of the start area (SUPPORT2) rendered pure black
          because the IWAD's E1M4 sidedefs reference textures in
          lowercase ("support2", "compspan", "plat1", "stone",
          "sladwall", "doortrak", "Bigdoor2", "Compute2", "ExitDoor",
          "doorstop"; also E1M2 "compute1"/"shawn2", E1M7 "step4",
          E1M8 "doorstop"/"doortrak"/"shawn2") while TEXTURE1 stores
          them UPPERCASE.  Vanilla W_CheckNumForName is case-
          insensitive; the port's Renderer.texture/flat compared with
          ==, so the lookup returned nil and the wall column loop
          drew nothing (black).  Fixed: added nameeq() (ASCII case
          fold) and switched Renderer.texture/flat + the ANIMATED
          anim-init compares to it.  Verified headless (TEMP harness,
          removed): all 14 lowercase/uppercase variant lookups
          resolve, TXTEST-PASS; boot smoke clean.  Visual confirm
          pending: restart, re-check the wall, snap if needed.
          Runtime quiet: audio driver DPRINT off (per-tic
          audio_file_write spam gone); the 70-tic status heartbeat
          in doom.b now prints only with -v.
          SOUND LATENCY (Oct 24): "slight delay" on effects traced
          to the libpulsedsp shim: apps that never set OSS fragment
          size get its default stream buffer of 12 x 1024 bytes =
          ~1.1s of PulseAudio-side buffering (padsp.c fix_metrics +
          create_playback_stream tlength=frag*nfrag).  Fixed in
          audio-oss.c audio_open: SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT with 4
          fragments of 256 bytes (non-fatal; real OSS drivers round
          it).  VERIFIED at the server: pactl Buffer Latency 86ms
          (was ~1.1s); Sink Latency 49ms is WSLg transport, fixed.
          Total onset latency now ~90+50+tic(~29)ms.  If heavy
          scenes ever underrun (crackle), raise n_fragments 4->8
          (~186ms margin) at the same call site.
          PERF ROUND 2 (Oct 24, choppy frames on E1M4): two hitch
          sources fixed.  (1) The per-tic 315-byte audio write
          BLOCKED the tic loop: the shim sizes its socket receive
          buffer to fragment_size (256B), so every write's last ~59
          bytes waited ~23ms for the next PulseAudio drain -- a
          guaranteed stall per tic.  Fragment changed to 512 bytes x
          2 fragments: the write fits the buffer (no block) and
          tlength stays ~93ms; verified Buffer Latency ~126ms.
          (2) drawmasked allocated its sprite sort array every frame
          (heap churn); now a preallocated MAXVISSPRITES array is
          reused (slice view, same backing store).  view.dis + emu
          rebuilt, boot smoke clean.  Pending: user smoothness test.
          Next perf candidates if needed: M24 blockmap (brute-force
          collision scans dominate E1M4 tic cost), per-frame alloc
          audit of view.b ring/snapshot paths (off by default).
          PERF ROUND 3 (Oct 24, after "still choppy"): added -p
          frame profiling (doom.b: tic/render/convert/present avg+max
          per 70 tics; view.b setperf(): bsp/mob/planes/sprites).
          Measured on E1M4 start: tic 0ms, render 19 (bsp 9, planes 8,
          sprites 1), convert 11, present ~1 -> frame ~31ms > 28.6ms
          tick period = constant 1-2 tic catch-up alternation (the
          judder), not low framerate.  Fixes: (1) convert 8x unroll
          with hoisted palette slice refs (11->9ms; still byte-store
          bound, ~64k px x 4 stores); (2) wall-column texture coord:
          col = floor(u/65536) == floor(ud/id) for ud>=0 -> plain
          32-bit division instead of big(); negative texture offsets
          keep the big path; frac int path when x-ox1<=32767;
          (3) row math fixedmul fast path per seg when |dz|<2^25 and
          max scale<2^22 (|dz*scale|<2^47 keeps fixedmul exact; walls
          closer than ~1.25u keep big).  CRITICAL BUG during this
          round: first fast-path omitted the final >>16 (fixedmul
          yields 16.16 rows; (dz*scale)>>32 is integer) -> walls
          rendered garbage; fixed and proven bit-exact with a TEMP
          Limbo oracle over 247 dz/scale cases incl. negatives,
          extremes and the guard boundary (ROWCHECK-PASS; harness
          removed).  (4) rdrawcolumn/drawspan hoist fb8/colormap/
          tex.data/flat.data slice refs out of the per-pixel loop and
          drawspan uses ((fy>>16)&63)<<6 instead of *64: planes
          8->3ms, render 19->15, frame 31->25-26ms avg, n=70 frames
          per 70 tics = exactly 1 tick/frame (judder gone).  Remaining
          headroom: convert ~9ms is the Limbo per-byte-store floor;
          max frames 31-37 still 2-tic when spikes hit; next big lever
          is M24 blockmap for tic cost (E1M4 brute-force AI scans).
          SWITCH BUG (Oct 24, E1M4 start switch): user reported the
          switch stays in its initial (down) state forever.  Root
          cause: changeswitchtexture matched only the FIRST member of
          each pair (a == texture, swap to b).  Vanilla
          P_ChangeSwitchTexture scans BOTH members (switchlist[i],
          i in 0..2*numswitches) and swaps to the partner, and the
          repeatable-switch button restores the CURRENT texture.  The
          E1M4 line (ld614, special 18, tag 6) starts on SW2BROWN -
          the second name - so it never matched and never swapped (the
          floor action itself fired; special cleared).  Fixed: match
          either member on top/mid/bottom, swap to the partner, and
          P_StartButton stores the matched texture.  Verified headless
          (TEMP harness, removed): press swaps SW2BROWN -> SW1BROWN,
          special cleared (permanent), SWTEST-PASS.
          PERF ROUND 4 - JIT (Oct 24, "choppy when enemies attack"):
          measured tick cost with 4 monster clusters awake/attacking
          (TEMP harness): 0.35-0.44 ms/tic - sim was NOT the cost.
          Full-frame probe: render 17-25ms (bsp 8-10, planes 5-9,
          sprites 1-2), convert 9ms -> 27-34ms/frame vs 28.6ms tick =
          judder in heavy views; span/column counts showed the room
          has 1896 spans / 988 wall cols (interpreter-bound).
          ROOT FIX: the emu has a Limbo->x86 JIT (`emu -c 1`), but
          segflush() in emu/Linux/segflush-386.c was a no-op, so
          modern NX kernels faulted on the first jump into compiled
          code ("sys: segmentation violation").  Implemented it
          properly (page-aligned mprotect PROT_READ|WRITE|EXEC).
          RESULT with -c 1: render 18-25ms -> 1-2ms, convert 9 ->
          0-1ms, whole frame ~31ms -> 2-3ms (10-15x), still exactly
          1 tick/frame.  CORRECTNESS: framebuffer hashes over 4
          viewpoints identical between interpreter and JIT
          (bit-exact render) and audio/switch/boot all verified under
          the JIT.  ~/bin/emu-audio now always passes -c 1 (comment
          documents the segflush dependency).  The earlier
          interpreter-path micro-optimisations from this round
          (convert unroll, wall col integer division, fixedmul row
          math, span/column field hoisting, sortord prealloc) are
          kept - all validated (ROWCHECK-PASS) and harmless.  -p
          output now also reports spans/cols/vp/vis/bbox/span-pixels
          per frame.
          PERF ROUND 5 - AUDIO DECOUPLING (Oct 24, "choppy only with
          audio on"): the game's per-tic 315-byte write to #A/audio
          went straight to the OSS device; the pulse OSS shim accepts
          data only in fragment bursts and its socket buffer is
          exactly one fragment, so writes blocked periodically and
          stalled the tic loop (judder with audio, none without).
          Fixed in emu/Linux/audio-oss.c with vanilla-style
          decoupling: audio_file_write copies into a 32KB ring
          (3s at 11025Hz) and a writer kproc drains the ring into
          the device, absorbing all blocking.  Ring overflow drops
          (cannot occur in practice); audio_file_close stops the
          writer (drop aq.lk around Sleep - Inferno Sleep does not
          release caller locks, first attempt deadlocked the tic
          loop) and the writer owns/closes the fd so a later open
          cannot race.  VERIFIED: game under emu-audio reports
          tick max 0ms, frame 3ms avg / 5-6ms max, exactly 1 tick
          per frame (n=70/70), pulse stream live at u8 1ch 11025Hz;
          a write probe showed 100/100 writes completing without
          stall and audio still plays.

          M18 STATUS BAR + VIEW SIZING (Oct 24): implemented to
          completion.  st.m/st.b (single-player path): full-bar
          redraw per frame instead of vanilla backing-screen diff
          blits (identical pixels; JIT makes redraw cheaper than
          diff tracking).  Widgets exactly per st_stuff.c/st_lib.c:
          ready-weapon tall ammo (1994 = no-ammo weapon draws
          nothing), health/% and armor/% tall widgets, STARMS
          background + six STGNUM/STYSNUM slots (gray = not owned,
          yellow = owned, vanilla 12x10 grid), face (STFB0 backdrop
          + vanilla ST_updateFaceWidget priority machine with
          M_Random's own rndtable index, pain offsets, turn
          left/right unsigned-angle compare, ouch/evilgrin/rampage,
          god/dead faces), three key boxes (cards 0-2 replaceable by
          skulls 3-5), four small ammo counts + maxima (y order
          173/179/191/185), and ST_doPaletteStuff (damage red 1-8,
          pickup gold 9-12, radiation 13, berserk fade; palette data
          moved into the Renderer adt because each module load is a
          separate instance - the first version read a never-
          initialised paldata and failed the palette tests).
          View sizing: viewheight = 200-32 = 168, Renderer keeps the
          320x200 fb8 (bar drawn over rows 168..199 after the world,
          before convert), centery = viewheight/2 = 84 exactly like
          R_ExecuteSetViewSize; projection unchanged (160).  F1
          snapshot still dumps all 200 fb rows (FB8 h = len/w).
          VALIDATION (all TEMP harnesses removed):
          (1) bar pixel oracle: deterministic state (pistol,
          200/50/300/50 ammo, all weapons+keys, 200 armor, 100 hp,
          face STFST00) vs a Python reconstruction of st_stuff/
          st_lib from the raw ST* patches: 0/10240 pixels differ.
          (2) face truth table via dumped-bar face identification
          (unique patch match): straight -> STFST02, attacker
          head-on -> STFKILL0 (painoffset+7), 60hp head-on ->
          STFKILL1 (+15), god mode -> STFGOD0, dead -> STFDEAD0:
          5/5.
          (3) palette shifts: idle/damage40(->6)/damage200(->8
          clamp)/bonus20(->12)/ironfeet(->13)/back-to-idle vs
          PLAYPAL rows read from the WAD: 6/6 PASS.
          (4) view sizing: 168-row render is byte-identical to the
          200-row render's rows 16..183 (same projection, horizon
          moved): VIEWTEST-PASS.
          Bug found during wiring: st.b originally loaded its own
          Game module instance (per-load-instance globals) whose
          tables were never init()ed; the damage-face path's
          pointtoangle2 would nil-deref in real play.  st->init now
          takes doom.b's live Game handle (same for the Renderer's
          palette data).  Boot smoke clean with audio at 3ms frames.
          Residuals: frags widget is deathmatch-only (not drawn);
          chat/message counter and automap palette branches are
          absent (automap is M20); the ~10MB F2 ring is preallocated
          even with -R off (found while instantiating two Views:
          heap limit) - lazy-allocate later if memory matters.
          M18 FOLLOW-UP FIXES (user reports):
          (1) Green backdrop behind the face: vanilla draws the STFB
          face background ONLY for netgames (player-color backdrop;
          st_stuff.c refreshes it under `if (netgame)`); single
          player shows the bar art.  The drawer had drawn it
          unconditionally.  Removed (STFB0 still loaded for a future
          netgame path).  NOTE: the earlier bar pixel oracle included
          the backdrop, so it validated the implementation against
          itself there - the oracle encoded the bug; flagged so
          future oracle work reads the vanilla guards, not just the
          widget math.
          (2) Permanent yellow (and red) screen tint after pickups/
          damage: vanilla P_PlayerThink decrements damagecount and
          bonuscount every tic (p_user.c:349-352) which drives the
          ST_doPaletteStuff shift down; the port never decremented
          them, so bonuscount stayed at BONUSADD(6) forever after any
          pickup -> the gold palette 10 never faded.  Added the two
          decrements in vanilla order (before the power-duration
          block).  Verified headless (TEMP harness removed): pickup
          flash 6 -> palette 10 -> fades to 0 in 6 tics; damage 40 ->
          palette 6 -> fades to 0; counts end at 0 - FLASHTEST-PASS.
          (3) "less of the gun sprite": the bar IS a separate 32-row
          band (world renders 0..167, bar 168..199 - vanilla
          screenblocks 10), but the psprite vertical anchor was
          wrong: vanilla builds texturemid from BASEYCENTER (screen
          100, r_things.c:41/687) while the draw offset uses
          centeryfrac (viewheight/2).  The port used centery for both,
          so at the 168 view the weapon sat (100-84)=16px too low -
          exactly 16 extra rows hidden behind the bar.  Fixed with a
          BASEYCENTER constant.  Verified (TEMP harness removed):
          with the weapon raised, the 168-row render equals the
          200-row render rows 16..183 byte-for-byte including the gun
          (PSPRITETEST-PASS; 13600 gun-area pixels both).
          (4) Audio delay returned after the decoupling ring: a
          catch-up burst (or a stalled frame) queues audio faster
          than the device drains, and since production == consumption
          in steady state the backlog never shrinks (the old blocking
          writes used to self-correct).  audio-oss.c aqenqueue now
          caps the queue at AQCAP = 4 game tics (~114ms) and drops the
          OLDEST audio on overflow, so latency stays bounded; boot
          verified with the pulse stream live at u8 1ch 11025Hz.
          (5) "No sound picking up soulsphere": investigated end to
          end - the pickup sound engine is correct (a probe playing
          SFX_GETPOW through the mixer rendered 4582 non-silent
          samples), and a walk-into-soulsphere harness shows health
          100->200, itemcount++ and the GETPOW channel started
          (vol 64).  One real fidelity bug found and fixed while
          verifying: pickup sounds were started with origin (0,0)
          (the "global" path) instead of vanilla's
          S_StartSound(player->mo).  Consequences of the old origin:
          (a) S_StopSound(origin) cut GLOBAL sounds (e.g. a door
          that was opening) whenever an item was picked up; (b) with
          origin 0 a pickup could never REUSE the player's own
          channel - vanilla's same-origin loop reuses the weapon-fire
          channel - so in a fight with all 8 channels holding
          high-priority monster sounds the GETPOW (priority 60) could
          be priority-dropped while vanilla's would still play.
          Fixed: pickup sounds now start at player.mo.x/y (same
          audible result - same-spot-as-listener is full volume - and
          vanilla's stop/reuse semantics).  Note: startsound uses the
          listener position from the previous tic (S_UpdateSounds
          runs at tic end); 1 tic of movement is ~1 unit, inaudible,
          and the only observable edge is a pickup touched on the
          very first tic after boot (listener still 0,0 -> dropped);
          not reachable in normal play.

          M19 HUD MESSAGES (Oct 24): implemented to completion
          (single-player).  hu.m/hu.b: HUD font STCFN033..095, one
          message line at (0,0) - vanilla HU_MSGHEIGHT is 1, so
          messages replace each other rather than stacking -
          HU_MSGTIMEOUT 4*TICRATE, uppercase folding with 4px gaps
          for space/unknown and edge clipping per HUlib_drawTextLine.
          Level title built at level start from the vanilla
          HUSTR_E1Mx table (d_englsh.h transcribed) and drawn at
          (0,167-fontheight) only while the automap is active
          (vanilla HU_Drawer gate; hu.setautomap is the M20 hook).
          Message storage follows vanilla: game.m Player gained a
          `message` string; every pickup site in ptouchspecialthing
          sets the exact GOT_* string (armor/mega/health/armor bonus/
          supercharge/keys/megahealth?, stim/medikit with the
          health<25 variant, powers, ammo types, backpack, weapons),
          locked doors set PD_*K ("You need a X key to open this
          door"), and hu.ticker consumes plr.message per tic like
          HU_Ticker.  Obituaries: this doomgeneric tree has no OB_*
          strings or death-message path at all, so they are out of
          scope (noted rather than invented).
          VALIDATION (TEMP harnesses removed): (1) end-to-end
          soulsphere pickup produces "Supercharge!" and the drawn
          text matches a Python font oracle pixel-for-pixel (0
          diffs over the glyph rows); (2) the title line "E1M4:
          Command Control" matches its oracle (0 diffs); (3) after
          141 tickers the message area is blank (timeout honours
          HU_MSGTIMEOUT=140); (4) using E1M4's blue-locked door with
          no key yields exactly "You need a blue key to open this
          door" (PD_BLUEK).  LEVEL-START detail: hu.start hides any
          on-screen message (vanilla HU_Start message_on=false) while
          keeping the pending text like vanilla.
          GOTCHA: adding Player.message changed the Game module type
          hash; all game.m consumers (view, wi, st, hu, doom, game)
          had to be rebuilt and installed together, otherwise module
          load fails the link typecheck.
          M20 AUTOMAP (Oct 24): implemented am_map.c's full-screen
          single-player path.  am.m/am.b: vertex-bound level init
          (vanilla's else-if quirk included), initial zoom
          min_scale/0.7 clamped to max, 16.16 map<->fb transforms,
          follow (FTOM(MTOF()) snap; m_x2/m_y2), pan (F_PANINC per
          tic, clamps to level bounds, clears follow), zoom multiplier
          chain (1.02 / 1/1.02 per tic with min/max-out clamps), max
          zoom toggle ('0' saves/restores scale+loc).  Drawing:
          AM_clearFB, exact Cohen-Sutherland AM_clipMline with C
          truncating division, exact Bresenham AM_drawFline, wall
          color rules (solid/teleporter/secret/floor/ceiling/two-sided
          cheating; GRAYS+3 all-map; ML_DONTDRAW hidden unless iddt),
          player arrow/iddt arrow, iddt thing triangles (correct
          thintriangle_guy table - the first cut mistakenly used
          triangle_guy's vertices and the pixel oracle caught it),
          blockmap-aligned grid (new Level.bmaporgx/bmaporgy from the
          BLOCKMAP lump header), AMMNUM marks and the center
          crosshair pixel.  Input/plumbing: TAB toggle; pan arrows
          only with follow off and consumed so movement doesn't also
          fire; '-'/'=' zoom; '0' max zoom; f/g/m/c with the vanilla
          AMSTR_* messages through player->message; keys routed only
          in GS_LEVEL; map replaces the world draw but the status bar,
          HUD messages and title still draw; AM_Stop on death and on
          GA_COMPLETED; AM_LevelInit per level.  ML_MAPPED marking was
          added to view.b at vanilla r_segs.c:395's spot (a stored
          wall range marks its linedef; fully occluded ranges return
          before it, as in vanilla).  iddt text input is deferred to
          M25; am.cheatcycle() advances 0->1->2.
          VALIDATION (TEMP harness and Python oracle removed): nine
          E1M4 modes - all-mapped walls, all-map power, grid, marks,
          zoom+pan, iddt walls, iddt things, follow snap and
          renderer-explored lines - plus E1M8 walls/allmap (83
          ML_DONTDRAW lines): every mode matched a from-source Python
          reimplementation pixel-for-pixel (0 diffs over 320x168).
          The explored mode used one real View::render frame and
          marked 28 linedefs, confirming the renderer->automap
          handoff.  Oracle bugs found and fixed during validation
          (last-lump WAD dict, signed 0xfffe sidenums) were oracle
          only; the port had exactly one real bug (triangle table).
          Boot smoke with audio passes; TAB key moves were not
          scriptable headless (module behavior fully validated in the
          harness).
          VIDEO SCALING + BOOT MAP (Oct 25): the game was fixed at
          320x200 in both video paths.  Video now scales its 320x200
          XRGB frame to the destination with nearest-neighbour
          sampling, preserving the 320:200 aspect and centring it
          (black letterbox): open() fills the emu screen, openwm()
          fills the resizable Tk panel.  Context gained sw/sh (source),
          winw/winh (destination), x0/y0/dw/dh (game rect), a dw*dh*4
          scaled buffer and per-column/row source offset maps;
          Context.resize re-lays-out for any destination size and (in
          wm mode) allocates/attaches the new panel image.  Present
          takes a direct writepixels fast path when the destination is
          exactly 320x200 at 0,0, otherwise scales.  The wm path polls
          the panel's actwidth/actheight once per present, so the
          titlebar's Resize button (Tkclient->Resize is now passed)
          scales the game live; new images are black so the letterbox
          is clean.  open() clears the emu screen once at startup so
          its letterbox is black too.
          doom.b: new -s N (wm window starts at 320N x 200N, default 2;
          ignored in full-screen mode) and -m ExMy (boot map; default
          remains E1M4; setuplevel derives gamemapnum from the name so
          progression/par time/secret-exit logic is unchanged).  The
          emu X11 window itself is fixed at startup (win-x11a sets
          PMaxSize; no ConfigureNotify handling), so ~/bin/emu-audio
          now passes -g ${EMU_GEOM:-1280x960} (a later user -g wins).
          VALIDATION (TEMP harness and oracle removed): Video.open and
          Video.openwm were driven with a deterministic BGRX pattern,
          then the destination images read back with readpixels and
          compared pixel-for-pixel against an oracle reimplementing the
          nearest-neighbour mapping and centring.  Cases: emu screen
          640x480 and 1280x960 (game 640x400 @0,40 and 1280x800 @0,80),
          forced relayouts 500x340 / 320x200 (fast path) / 639x479,
          wm panel 638x400 (initial) and a live wm resize to a 698x476
          panel (requested 700x500 through the real wm channel, picked
          up by present's poll).  All zero differing pixels.
          Timings (JIT, -p): standalone 1280x960 pres ~15ms avg/max 22,
          frame ~18ms avg (35Hz budget 28.6ms); wm 640x400 pres ~1-3ms,
          frame ~5-7ms; -s 3 similar.  Standalone with audio still
          boots clean after the wrapper change.  -m E1M5 verified to
          boot E1M5 with the player spawned correctly.
          VIDEO SCALING FOLLOW-UP (Oct 25): the first openwm API passed
          the window size where the source (game frame) dimensions
          belonged.  In wm mode the scaler therefore treated the
          320x200 frame as 640x400, interleaving pairs of game rows and
          producing the "two horizontally duplicated copies" seen live.
          The first scaling harness shared the same mistake (it filled a
          640x400 source), which is why it passed.  openwm now takes
          sw/sh (the 320x200 game frame) plus width/height (window
          size); doom.b passes both.  Re-validated with a corrected
          harness on a real wm: panel 640x400 at 2x (fast 1:1 source
          mapping) and a live wm resize to a 898x676 panel (game
          898x561 centred at y=57), both pixel-exact against the
          320x200 pattern oracle.  The standalone path was unaffected.
          wm -p at 1280x960 screen: render 3ms, pres 3ms, frame 7ms.

